# Committed readings for wrong-kind-traps.wasm (#1282, item 3): each built-in given a handle that is not of its kind.
# Built:       wasm-tools parse wrong-kind-traps.wat -o wrong-kind-traps.wasm   (wasm-tools 1.258.0; validates --features all)
# Invocation:  wasmtime run -W component-model-async=y -S p3=y --invoke '<export>()' wrong-kind-traps.wasm
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime, darwin/arm64.
# Runs:        10 of 10 per line, each identical. Taken 2026-11-21.
#
# Each line is `<export>: <wasmtime's reading>`, a trap's last line of stderr and its exit status. Every row traps in
# the pin too, at the built-in's own `trap_if(not isinstance(...))`, or at def:Table.get for an index with no entry.
stream-read-writable: trap: wasm trap: cannot have concurrent operations active on a future/stream (exit 134)
stream-write-readable: trap: wasm trap: cannot have concurrent operations active on a future/stream (exit 134)
stream-cancel-read-writable: trap: passed write end to `{stream|future}.cancel-read` (exit 1)
stream-cancel-write-readable: trap: passed read end to `{stream|future}.cancel-write` (exit 1)
stream-drop-readable-writable: trap: handle is not a readable end of a stream (exit 1)
stream-drop-writable-readable: trap: passed read end to `stream.drop-writable` (exit 1)
stream-write-no-handle: trap: unknown handle index 99 (exit 1)
future-read-writable: trap: wasm trap: cannot have concurrent operations active on a future/stream (exit 134)
future-read-stream: trap: handle is not a future (exit 1)
future-write-readable: trap: wasm trap: cannot have concurrent operations active on a future/stream (exit 134)
future-cancel-write-readable: trap: passed read end to `{stream|future}.cancel-write` (exit 1)
future-drop-readable-writable: trap: handle is not a readable end of a future (exit 1)
join-set-as-waitable: trap: handle is not a waitable (exit 1)
join-end-as-set: trap: handle is not an waitable-set (exit 1)
wait-end: trap: handle is not an waitable-set (exit 1)
poll-end: trap: handle is not an waitable-set (exit 1)
set-drop-end: trap: handle is not a waitable-set (exit 1)
subtask-cancel-end: trap: handle is not a subtask (exit 1)
subtask-drop-end: trap: handle is not a subtask (exit 1)
#
# Meaning, with the pin's line for each.
#   stream-*, future-*  def:copy, def:cancel_copy and def:drop each open with "trap_if(not isinstance(end, EndT))",
#                     so the other end of the same stream or future traps, as does a stream end given to a future
#                     built-in (future-read-stream). wasmtime's four copy rows trap with a different reason,
#                     "cannot have concurrent operations active", which is where its read and write of the wrong end
#                     stop; it is a trap, as the pin's is.
#   stream-write-no-handle  index 99 names no entry, def:Table.get:"trap_if(i >= len(self.array))".
#   join-*            def:canon_waitable_join: "trap_if(not isinstance(w, Waitable))" for a waitable set as `wi`, and
#                     "trap_if(not isinstance(wset, WaitableSet))" for a stream end as `si`.
#   wait-, poll-, set-drop-end  def:canon_waitable_set_wait, def:canon_waitable_set_poll and
#                     def:canon_waitable_set_drop: "trap_if(not isinstance(wset, WaitableSet))".
#   subtask-*         def:canon_subtask_cancel:"trap_if(not isinstance(subtask, Subtask))" and
#                     def:canon_subtask_drop:"trap_if(not isinstance(s, Subtask))".
#
# Burroughs before #1282 (main at d8076a1): every row returns a Go error rather than a trap, "component: <built-in>:
# handle N is not a <kind>". Burroughs since: a trap reading "<built-in>: handle N is not a <kind> (the pin's
# def:<symbol>: trap_if(not isinstance(...)))".
